@namespace XgFilter_Razor.Components.Internal
@attribute [System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsable(System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsableState.Never)]
@using System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis
@using System.Text.Json
@using XgFilter_Lib.Filtering
@using XgFilter_Lib.Enums
@using XgFilter_Lib.Patterns
@using BgDataTypes_Lib
@inject IJSRuntime JS

@* ── FilterPanel.razor ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   NOT consumer surface. Hosts embed FilterSurface, which owns this panel and
   the interaction wiring; the .Internal namespace + EditorBrowsable(Never)
   are the strongest narrowing the Razor toolchain allows (a true `internal`
   component draws CS0262 — the generator hardcodes `public partial`; spike
   result recorded in INSTRUCTIONS Pitfalls). Consuming it from a host is
   banned outright — including host tests (no FindComponent<FilterPanel>).
   Owns all filter state. Raises OnFilterConfigChanged only when "Apply Filter"
   is clicked (or "Clear filters"). Raises OnAppliedStateChanged after every
   buffer-affecting gesture, carrying the committed config the buffers now
   equal (null when they equal none), so the parent can gate downstream actions
   on whether the panel's selection is still the one it acted on. That same
   truth gates Apply itself: re-applying an unchanged selection is not offered.
   Consumers that need a DecisionFilterSet call FilterConfig.Build() on the
   received config.
   Information hierarchy: the error-range section is first and always visible;
   each of the other eight facets is its own collapsible row, one tier, in the
   lib's FilterFacet declaration order, and all eight rows sit behind one
   further disclosure — the container whose toggle names itself from
   MoreFiltersFoldedLabel / MoreFiltersExpandedLabel — so the panel at rest is
   the error range and the two buttons. That container reads as the rows'
   parent rather than as a ninth row: it carries header weight, and its region
   indents the rows under it. A collapsed row names its facet and, while that
   facet holds an active filter, carries a badge; the folded container carries
   the count of those — see the facet-row and container comments for how
   presence and wording divide.
   State persists to localStorage under three keys: the whole filter selection
   as one serialized FilterConfig blob (lib-owned shape via FilterConfig.ToJson
   / TryFromJson), the set of expanded rows under its own key (DisclosureKey),
   and whether the container is open under its own (MoreFiltersKey) — the last
   two user preference, never part of the config blob. Every storage call is
   guarded (halheinrich/backgammon#102): a browser that refuses storage leaves
   the panel working from its defaults and the host told once through
   OnStorageUnavailable — see the storage seam below the restores. A stored
   selection the panel cannot read restores to defaults and says so
   (#filterRestoreFailedNotice): a failed restore is never silent.
   Location: XgFilter_Razor/Components/FilterPanel.razor
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@

<div class="card mb-4" style="max-width:800px">
    <div class="card-header">
        <strong>Filters</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">

        @* ── Restored-selection notice (§4 legibility) ───────────────────────
           A reload ends the setup: selections are restored, applied-ness is
           not, Apply re-arms — correct by rule, but indistinguishable from a
           bug unless the screen says what happened. Shown while the
           app-scoped RestoreNotice is armed (this boot's restore staged a
           stored selection and no gesture has superseded it); it survives
           navigation with the state it describes and dies at the first
           buffer-affecting gesture or commit — or when the user closes it,
           which is the same death (see FilterRestoreNotice.Dismiss). A polite
           outcome, not an error.

           An event notice, so dismissible (the umbrella's SPEC-notices.md), and
           its occurrence outlives this component, so the dismissal is
           caller-held: bound to the app-scoped holder, which is why a
           navigate-back cannot bring a closed notice back. No @if around it and
           no bit here — the holder's IsVisible is the one fact, read once; the
           component renders nothing while it is false, whether the notice was
           never armed or is over. *@
        <Notice id="filterRestoredNotice" class="mb-3"
                Kind="NoticeKind.Information" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite"
                Dismissible="true"
                @bind-Dismissed:get="!RestoreNotice.IsVisible"
                @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => RestoreNotice.Dismiss()">
            This filter was restored from a previous session — it isn't in
            effect until you press <strong>Apply Filter</strong>.
        </Notice>

        @* ── Failed-restore notice ────────────────────────────────────────────
           The restore's other outcome (halheinrich/backgammon#367): a stored
           selection this panel cannot read restores to defaults, and defaults
           with no word are indistinguishable from a first visit — the user's
           filter gone for no stated reason. So the panel says so. Only for a
           document that is present and unreadable: nothing stored is nothing
           failed, and a storage call that threw is the storage-unavailable
           condition, reported to the host instead (OnStorageUnavailable).

           An event notice — this boot's restore failed once — so dismissible
           (the umbrella's SPEC-notices.md), and its occurrence outlives this
           component exactly as the restored-selection notice's does: the
           unreadable document survives navigation and a remount reads it
           again. So the dismissal is the same app-scoped holder's, its second
           fact, and the binding is the box above's shape — no @if, no bit
           here. It ends when the user closes it or a commit's write lands,
           replacing the unreadable document; an edit leaves it standing, and
           so does a commit whose write the browser refused, since neither
           changes what is stored. Polite: an outcome to understand, not a
           gesture that failed. *@
        <Notice id="filterRestoreFailedNotice" class="mb-3"
                Kind="NoticeKind.Warning" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite"
                Dismissible="true"
                @bind-Dismissed:get="!RestoreNotice.IsFailureVisible"
                @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => RestoreNotice.DismissFailure()">
            Your remembered filter couldn't be read, so the filters start from
            their defaults.
        </Notice>

        @* ── Error range ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
           First and always visible: dogfooding showed it is the panel's
           most-used control, so it never rides behind a row of its own.

           Validity is the lib's ruling, not this panel's: GetInvalidFields()
           names the offending bounds (non-negative, min ≤ max, NaN rejected)
           and each input reds itself on its own FilterField, so a wrong max
           never marks a min the user got right — the lib's attribution rules
           decide that, and a misordered pair blames both by design. Hoisted
           into a local like the active-facet set below: every marked input
           and every feedback line on the panel — this facet's, the score
           tokens', the move numbers', the pattern's — reads that one
           evaluation, and the Apply gate reads the same rule through CanApply.
           The panel writes no rule of its own here — only the words for it.

           How an input wears the verdict is rendered from one place too
           (FieldAttributes, halheinrich/backgammon#270): the is-invalid class,
           aria-invalid, and an aria-describedby that names the field's hint
           always and its rendered feedback line only while that line exists.
           Every text and number box on the panel splats that one result, so a
           seventh field cannot carry the class and miss the announcement. *@
        @{ var invalidFields = InvalidFields; }
        <div class="mb-3">
            @* The heading and its hint carry ids of their own because the two
               boxes below name themselves by reference off them
               (halheinrich/backgammon#196), exactly as a row's controls name
               themselves off the row's toggle and hint. The id is on the
               heading WORDS rather than on the label element around them: a
               reference to the whole label would pull the hint into the name
               and make "(inclusive; blank = no bound)" part of what the box is
               called, which is the lie the position-pattern field's own
               comment records dropping. A `for`-label per input was the other
               way offered and is refused for the same reason it was there:
               one label cannot serve two boxes, and splitting it into visible
               Min / Max labels would redesign the row to say what the
               placeholders already say. The heading itself is the lib's label
               via ToLabel() now that it is what a screen reader reads out —
               the rows' rule, which this section was the last literal to keep
               outside. *@
            <label class="form-label">
                <span id="errorRangeLabel">@FilterFacet.ErrorRange.ToLabel()</span>
                <small class="text-muted" id="errorRangeHint">(inclusive; blank = no bound)</small>
            </label>
            @* Both range facets carry the same Min/Max placeholders, so the
               boxes carry ids: a handle that says which facet it belongs to,
               rather than one that resolves by whichever section renders
               first. *@
            <div class="d-flex gap-2 align-items-center">
                <input id="errorMin"
                       @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(invalidFields, FilterField.ErrorMin, "errorRangeHint", "errorRangeFeedback"))"
                       style="max-width:140px"
                       aria-labelledby="errorRangeLabel"
                       type="number" step="0.001" placeholder="Min"
                       value="@_draft.ErrorMinText"
                       @oninput="e => Edit(_draft with { ErrorMinText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })" />
                <span>–</span>
                <input id="errorMax"
                       @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(invalidFields, FilterField.ErrorMax, "errorRangeHint", "errorRangeFeedback"))"
                       style="max-width:140px"
                       aria-labelledby="errorRangeLabel"
                       type="number" step="0.001" placeholder="Max"
                       value="@_draft.ErrorMaxText"
                       @oninput="e => Edit(_draft with { ErrorMaxText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })" />
            </div>
            @* Rendered only while it applies, the #applyDisabledReason idiom,
               rather than left in the DOM for Bootstrap's sibling selector to
               reveal: the inputs sit one level down inside the flex row, so
               `.is-invalid ~ .invalid-feedback` cannot reach across — hence the
               explicit d-block and the @if. Since halheinrich/backgammon#270
               every feedback line on the panel renders this way, because the
               input's aria-describedby names it while it is invalid and a
               reference to a hidden element is read out regardless.
               One line for both violations, because the
               user fixes them at the same two boxes: "a number, zero or
               greater" is also the true verdict on the literal NaN that
               double.TryParse accepts and the lib rejects.
               Keyed to this facet's own two fields, never to "the lib named
               something": the invalid set spans facets (the score tokens
               joined it in halheinrich/backgammon#121), and a line that fired
               on any membership would explain the error bounds over a fault
               the user committed three sections down. *@
            @if (invalidFields.Contains(FilterField.ErrorMin)
                 || invalidFields.Contains(FilterField.ErrorMax))
            {
                <div id="errorRangeFeedback" class="invalid-feedback d-block">
                    Each bound must be a number, zero or greater, and Min can't be
                    above Max — fix it to apply, or clear a bound for no limit on
                    that side.
                </div>
            }
        </div>

        @* ── Facet rows ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
           One collapsible row per offered facet, one tier, inside the More
           filters container below and in the lib's FilterFacet declaration
           order. Each is an honest disclosure in the level-group idiom one
           tier up: a real button carrying aria-expanded / aria-controls over
           the always-rendered #facet_<Facet> region, whose children render
           only while expanded (absent from the DOM when collapsed, not styled
           away), plus a decorative +/− glyph that is aria-hidden because the
           button's own aria-expanded already says which way the row sits. The
           button's accessible name is the facet's own label — the lib's
           FilterFacet [Description] via ToLabel(), never a literal here.
           Every id here is built from the facet's enum member name
           (facetToggle_, facet_, facetBadge_, and facetHint_ where a row's
           hint is referenced), one mould, so a control inside a row can name
           itself by pointing at its header instead of repeating the heading —
           which is exactly what the position-pattern field does.

           The badge divides in two, deliberately. Its PRESENCE is the lib's
           ruling: a row badges exactly when GetActiveFacets() names its facet,
           asked of the live edit buffers through the same build path Apply
           commits through (BuildConfig), never by re-inspecting a buffer or a
           config field here — the activation predicates are the lib's SSOT,
           and reading a buffer directly would be a second encoding of one of
           them. Reading the live buffers is also what keeps it honest at rest
           (after restore, Apply, Clear filters and LoadConfig staging) and
           live mid-edit. Its WORDS are the panel's display concern and nothing
           else's — see FacetBadgeText. It renders only while the row is
           collapsed: expanded, the controls themselves say everything the
           badge could, which is the level groups' ruling one tier down.

           Which rows are open is the user's choice, not the config's: the set
           persists under DisclosureKey, and LoadConfig / Clear filters never
           move it. Opening or closing a row is navigation, not an edit — no
           ReportAppliedState. The container below keeps its own open state the
           same way, under its own key; a row the user left open is still open
           when the container is next opened, whether or not the container was
           folded over it in between.

           Spacing is the row's, and it belongs to the BODY rather than to the
           row (halheinrich/backgammon#239). The rows pack tightly against one
           another — a stack of one-line headers reads as one list, and the
           blank line the wrapper's mb-3 used to draw between them made eight
           siblings look like eight sections. What still needs a gap beneath it
           is an expanded body, so the next row's header does not sit against
           the controls above it; so the step rides on the #facet_<Facet>
           region and only while that region has children. Conditional, not
           unconditional: the region is rendered in both states, and a margin
           on the empty one would put the blank line straight back between
           collapsed rows. One step throughout — mb-3, the panel's own. *@
        @{
            var activeFacets = ActiveFacets;

            RenderFragment FacetRow(FilterFacet facet, RenderFragment body)
            {
                var expanded = _expandedFacets.Contains(facet);
                var badge = !expanded && activeFacets.Contains(facet)
                    ? FacetBadgeText(facet)
                    : null;

                return @<div>
                    <button type="button" id="facetToggle_@facet"
                            class="btn btn-link btn-sm p-0 text-decoration-none"
                            aria-expanded="@(expanded ? "true" : "false")"
                            aria-controls="facet_@facet"
                            @onclick="() => ToggleFacetAsync(facet)">
                        <span aria-hidden="true">@(expanded ? "−" : "+")</span>
                        @facet.ToLabel()
                        @if (badge is not null)
                        {
                            <span id="facetBadge_@facet" class="badge text-bg-primary ms-1">@badge</span>
                        }
                    </button>
                    <div id="facet_@facet" class="@(expanded ? "mb-3" : null)">
                        @if (expanded)
                        {
                            @body
                        }
                    </div>
                </div>;
            }
        }

        @* ── More / Fewer filters ─────────────────────────────────────────────
           One disclosure over all eight rows (halheinrich/backgammon#231), so
           the panel at rest is the error range and the two buttons — the two
           things a returning user reaches for — and everything else is one
           click away. The error range and the Apply/Clear row stay outside it:
           they are what the fold is measured against.

           The rows' own idiom, one tier up: a real button carrying
           aria-expanded / aria-controls over the always-rendered #moreFilters
           region, whose children render only while expanded (absent from the
           DOM when folded, not styled away), plus the same aria-hidden +/−
           glyph. Its ids are the rows' mould without the per-facet suffix a
           singleton has no use for — moreFiltersToggle, moreFilters,
           moreFiltersBadge — and deliberately NOT facetToggle_/facet_/
           facetBadge_ shaped: those prefixes are surveyed as "the rows", here
           and in both hosts, and a container answering to them would
           misreport itself as a ninth row.

           Folded is the resting state, the posture the hierarchy ruling asks
           for one tier down (every row starts collapsed) and the whole reason
           this container exists: a panel that folded only after a click would
           not fold. What folding hides, the badge says — the number of rows
           whose facet is set, counted off the same ActiveFacets ruling the
           rows' own badges read (the lib's GetActiveFacets on the live
           buffers), never a second count of this panel's own. ErrorRange can
           be active and is never counted here: it is not behind this fold.
           Like a row's, the badge speaks only while folded — open, the rows'
           own badges say it in more detail.

           Its open state is the user's preference and persists like the rows',
           but under its own key (MoreFiltersKey) rather than joining the
           DisclosureKey set: that set's vocabulary is FilterFacet member names
           and this container is not a facet, so a sentinel name in it would be
           exactly the corruption its all-or-nothing restore exists to refuse.
           LoadConfig and Clear filters never move it, as with the rows —
           opening or closing it is navigation, not an edit, so no
           ReportAppliedState.

           Its hierarchy is drawn, not merely meant (halheinrich/backgammon#239).
           Drawn flat in the candidate the user rejected — nothing shipped that
           way — this control was measured expanded at the rows' own
           left edge, size, weight and colour, one 24px rhythm with no gap: the
           thing that folds the panel read as the first of nine identical links,
           told apart only by its glyph. Two marks fix that and both are
           structural rather than decorative. The REGION indents its rows as a
           group — ms-3, the same step a row's own body already takes under its
           header, so the panel says "inside" one way at both tiers rather than
           inventing a second. The TOGGLE takes header weight: fw-bold, the
           card header's <strong> in utility form, and it drops btn-sm, which
           was the rows' tier borrowed — the small scale is what marks the
           repeated tier, and the control over that tier is not of it. That
           lands it at the card body's own font size, bold, a tier above the
           14px regular of the rows it governs. No colour is added: btn-link's
           blue already says "activates", and a second colour here would be a
           second meaning to learn. It stays a button, not a heading element:
           what outline this panel sits in is the host's to know (FilterHelp
           takes a HeadingLevel for exactly that reason) and a hard-coded <h_>
           here would malform an outline this component cannot see.

           The gap after the toggle is the region's too, and conditional for
           the rows' reason one tier down: mt-3 while it has children, nothing
           while it is folded, so the resting panel keeps the tight line
           between this control and the buttons below.

           Its NAME flips with the fold — MoreFiltersFoldedLabel while folded,
           MoreFiltersExpandedLabel while open — so the control says what the
           next click does rather than describing a state the user is already
           looking at. The two words and the rule that picks between them live
           together beside MoreFiltersKey and nowhere else: the markup renders
           MoreFiltersLabel, FilterHelp renders the pair, and neither retypes
           either — the same one-owner rule the row headers keep by rendering
           ToLabel(). The accessible name follows the visible label because it
           IS the visible label: the button's own text, with only the glyph
           held out of it as aria-hidden. *@
        @{ var setRowCount = RowFacets.Count(activeFacets.Contains); }
        <div class="mb-3">
            <button type="button" id="moreFiltersToggle"
                    class="btn btn-link p-0 text-decoration-none fw-bold"
                    aria-expanded="@(_moreFiltersExpanded ? "true" : "false")"
                    aria-controls="moreFilters"
                    @onclick="ToggleMoreFiltersAsync">
                <span aria-hidden="true">@(_moreFiltersExpanded ? "−" : "+")</span>
                @MoreFiltersLabel
                @if (!_moreFiltersExpanded && setRowCount > 0)
                {
                    <span id="moreFiltersBadge" class="badge text-bg-primary ms-1">@setRowCount set</span>
                }
            </button>
            <div id="moreFilters" class="ms-3 @(_moreFiltersExpanded ? "mt-3" : string.Empty)">
                @if (_moreFiltersExpanded)
                {
                    @* ── Player ──────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.Players, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted" id="facetHint_Players">(comma-separated; blank = all players)</small>
                        </label>
                        <input class="form-control"
                               aria-labelledby="facetToggle_Players"
                               aria-describedby="facetHint_Players"
                               placeholder="e.g. Hal, Magriel"
                               value="@_draft.PlayersText"
                               @oninput="e => Edit(_draft with { PlayersText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })" />
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Decision type ───────────────────────────────────────
                       The one offered facet with no bracketed hint: its three options say
                       everything a hint would, so the row's body carries the controls
                       alone rather than an empty label element. *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.DecisionType, @<div class="ms-3">
                        @foreach (var opt in Enum.GetValues<DecisionTypeOption>())
                        {
                            <div class="form-check form-check-inline">
                                <input class="form-check-input" type="radio"
                                       name="decisionType"
                                       id="dt_@opt"
                                       checked="@(_draft.DecisionType == opt)"
                                       @onchange="() => Edit(_draft with { DecisionType = opt })" />
                                <label class="form-check-label" for="dt_@opt">
                                    @opt.ToLabel()
                                </label>
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Match score ─────────────────────────────────────────
                       The position-pattern field's shape, over a facet whose rule the
                       panel does not own: the lib names FilterField.MatchScores when any
                       entry is a token its grammar rejects, the input reds itself on that
                       membership, and the same IsCommittable member closes Apply — so a
                       mistyped or retired score can never silently drop the filter the
                       user meant to set.

                       Two verdicts, not one, because the remedies differ: a malformed
                       token is retyped, while a *retired* one was spelled correctly for
                       an earlier grammar and needs its replacements named. The lib draws
                       that distinction wordlessly (MatchScoreToken.GetFault) and the
                       panel words it — the GetInvalidFields / BoardPattern.TryParse
                       division of labour, one tier finer.

                       Every token spelling on this screen — placeholder, hint, and both
                       verdicts — renders from MatchScoreToken's exported constants. The
                       panel never types 'moneyJ' as a literal: the grammar that accepts
                       the token and the copy that advertises it are one source, which is
                       the whole point of the constants being exported
                       (halheinrich/backgammon#121). *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.MatchScores, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted" id="facetHint_MatchScores">(comma-separated; blank = all scores)</small>
                        </label>
                        <input @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(invalidFields, FilterField.MatchScores, "facetHint_MatchScores", "matchScoreFeedback"))"
                               aria-labelledby="facetToggle_MatchScores"
                               placeholder="e.g. 4a5a, 1a1a, @MatchScoreToken.DoubleMatchPoint, 1a2aC, @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithJacoby, @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithoutJacoby"
                               value="@_draft.MatchScoresText"
                               @oninput="e => Edit(_draft with { MatchScoresText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })" />
                        <div class="form-text">
                            MaNa is on-roll-anchored — M = points the player on roll needs, N = the
                            opponent — so 4a5a and 5a4a are distinct (list both to catch either
                            orientation). C suffix = Crawford game;
                            @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithJacoby / @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithoutJacoby =
                            a session played with / without the Jacoby rule;
                            @MatchScoreToken.DoubleMatchPoint is accepted as a spelling of 1a1a.
                        </div>
                        @* Same d-block + @if as the error-range line and for the same
                           reason — the input is a sibling here, but rendering the verdict
                           only while it applies keeps one idiom for the panel's feedback.
                           The faults are read only inside the branch: asking them costs a
                           buffer flush plus a sweep of the list, and outside the branch
                           the answer is always "none". *@
                        @if (invalidFields.Contains(FilterField.MatchScores))
                        {
                            var scoreFaults = MatchScoreFaults;
                            <div id="matchScoreFeedback" class="invalid-feedback d-block">
                                @if (scoreFaults.Contains(MatchScoreTokenFault.Malformed))
                                {
                                    <span>
                                        Not a valid score — write MaNa like 4a5a, add C for a
                                        Crawford game, or use @MatchScoreToken.DoubleMatchPoint,
                                        @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithJacoby or
                                        @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithoutJacoby; fix it to apply, or
                                        clear it for no score filter.
                                    </span>
                                }
                                @if (scoreFaults.Contains(MatchScoreTokenFault.Retired))
                                {
                                    <span>
                                        The @MatchScoreToken.RetiredMoney token is retired — a
                                        session now names the Jacoby rule it was played under: use
                                        @(string.Join(" or ", MatchScoreToken.RetiredMoneyReplacements)),
                                        or list both to admit either.
                                    </span>
                                }
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Move number range ───────────────────────────────────
                       The error range's sibling, wired identically because it is
                       the same shape of control under the same division of labour:
                       the lib rules on these bounds too, GetInvalidFields names the
                       offending one, each input reds itself on its own FilterField,
                       and a misordered pair blames both while neither bound is
                       wrong alone. It reads the hoisted invalidFields evaluation
                       the error range reads, and closes Apply through the same
                       IsCommittable member — no rule of the panel's own, here or
                       there. The facets differ only in the floor each bound must
                       clear (zero for an error magnitude, one for a 1-based move
                       ordinal), and the floor is the lib's to set; the panel only
                       words it. *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted" id="facetHint_MoveNumberRange">(inclusive; blank = no bound; non-standard-start games are excluded)</small>
                        </label>
                        <div class="d-flex gap-2 align-items-center">
                            <input id="moveNumberMin"
                                   @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(invalidFields, FilterField.MoveNumberMin, "facetHint_MoveNumberRange", "moveNumberFeedback"))"
                                   style="max-width:140px"
                                   aria-labelledby="facetToggle_MoveNumberRange"
                                   type="number" step="1" min="1" placeholder="Min"
                                   value="@_draft.MoveNumberMinText"
                                   @oninput="e => Edit(_draft with { MoveNumberMinText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })" />
                            <span>–</span>
                            <input id="moveNumberMax"
                                   @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(invalidFields, FilterField.MoveNumberMax, "facetHint_MoveNumberRange", "moveNumberFeedback"))"
                                   style="max-width:140px"
                                   aria-labelledby="facetToggle_MoveNumberRange"
                                   type="number" step="1" min="1" placeholder="Max"
                                   value="@_draft.MoveNumberMaxText"
                                   @oninput="e => Edit(_draft with { MoveNumberMaxText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })" />
                        </div>
                        @* #errorRangeFeedback's shape, for the same reasons: the
                           explicit @if and d-block because the inputs sit one level
                           down inside the flex row and Bootstrap's sibling selector
                           cannot reach across, and one line for both violations
                           because the user fixes either at the same two boxes.
                           Keyed to this facet's own two fields, never to "the lib
                           named something" — the invalid set spans facets, and a
                           line that fired on any membership would explain the move
                           numbers over a fault the user committed in another row.
                           The floor is stated here and nowhere else on this screen.
                           The hint at the head of this row's body says what the boxes
                           *mean* (which end is which, what blank does, which games the
                           facet can speak about at all); this says what they will
                           *accept*, and only while something is not accepted. The
                           error range splits its own copy on exactly that line —
                           "(inclusive; blank = no bound)" above, "zero or greater"
                           down here — so a reader who learns one facet's boxes
                           knows where to look for the other's. *@
                        @if (invalidFields.Contains(FilterField.MoveNumberMin)
                             || invalidFields.Contains(FilterField.MoveNumberMax))
                        {
                            <div id="moveNumberFeedback" class="invalid-feedback d-block">
                                Each bound must be a whole number, one or greater, and
                                Min can't be above Max — fix it to apply, or clear a
                                bound for no limit on that side.
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Contact type ────────────────────────────────────────── *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.ContactTypes, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted">(none checked = all types)</small>
                        </label>
                        <div>
                            @foreach (var ct in Enum.GetValues<ContactType>())
                            {
                                <div class="form-check form-check-inline">
                                    <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox"
                                           id="ct_@ct"
                                           checked="@_draft.ContactTypes.Contains(ct)"
                                           @onchange="(e) => Edit(_draft.WithContactType(ct, e.Value is true))" />
                                    <label class="form-check-label" for="ct_@ct">@ct.ToLabel()</label>
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Analysis depth ──────────────────────────────────────
                       Union-of-clauses facet over BgDataTypes_Lib's AnalysisMode ×
                       AnalysisLevel: three per-mode pairs, each a mode toggle plus its own
                       level group. The panel binds the six raw-intent inputs (three bools,
                       three level sets) verbatim and derives nothing — the mapping to
                       AnalysisDepthFilter clauses (one clause per enabled toggle, empty
                       level list = any level, all toggles off = facet off, inert level
                       lists) lives only in FilterConfig.Build(); re-encoding it here would
                       duplicate the SSOT and silently drift — see this panel's Pitfalls and
                       XgFilter_Lib's Depth-facet block. Mode labels come from AnalysisMode's
                       [Description]s via EnumLabel.ToLabel, never hardcoded. Levels render
                       in Enum.GetValues declaration order (the lib's ascending-rigor order);
                       Unknown is a first-class, selectable level.

                       Each level group is an honest disclosure in the facet rows' idiom one
                       tier down — real button, aria-expanded / aria-controls, children
                       rendered only while expanded — but deliberately UNPERSISTED, unlike
                       the rows' own DisclosureKey set: while collapsed, the count badge
                       ("any" / "N selected") already carries everything the closed state
                       hides, so remembering the open state buys no information and would
                       cost three more localStorage entries. The badge reads the live
                       buffer, so it is honest mid-edit and after restore / LoadConfig /
                       Clear. Collapsing or expanding a group is navigation, not an edit —
                       no ReportAppliedState, exactly as for the row above it.

                       Unchecking a mode hides its level group but keeps the checked levels
                       in the buffer (and in the emitted config): the lib guarantees a level
                       list whose toggle is off is inert — no activation, no constraint — so
                       re-toggling the mode restores the user's selection instead of
                       punishing an exploratory untoggle. *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.AnalysisDepth, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted">(check the modes you want to see — checking more shows more; nothing checked means no depth filter)</small>
                        </label>
                        @foreach (var group in DepthGroups)
                        {
                            <div>
                                <div class="form-check">
                                    <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox"
                                           id="md_@group.Mode"
                                           checked="@_draft.Includes(group.Mode)"
                                           @onchange="(e) => Edit(_draft.WithIncludes(group.Mode, e.Value is true))" />
                                    <label class="form-check-label" for="md_@group.Mode">@group.Mode.ToLabel()</label>
                                </div>
                                @if (_draft.Includes(group.Mode))
                                {
                                    <div class="ms-4 mb-2">
                                        <button type="button" id="lvlToggle_@group.Mode"
                                                class="btn btn-link btn-sm p-0 text-decoration-none"
                                                aria-expanded="@(group.LevelsExpanded ? "true" : "false")"
                                                aria-controls="lvl_@group.Mode"
                                                @onclick="() => group.LevelsExpanded = !group.LevelsExpanded">
                                            Analysis level
                                            @if (!group.LevelsExpanded)
                                            {
                                                var levelCount = _draft.LevelsOf(group.Mode).Count;
                                                <span id="lvlBadge_@group.Mode"
                                                      class="badge ms-1 @(levelCount > 0 ? "text-bg-primary" : "text-bg-secondary")">
                                                    @(levelCount > 0 ? $"{levelCount} selected" : "any")
                                                </span>
                                            }
                                        </button>
                                        <div id="lvl_@group.Mode">
                                            @if (group.LevelsExpanded)
                                            {
                                                <div>
                                                    @foreach (var level in Enum.GetValues<AnalysisLevel>())
                                                    {
                                                        <div class="form-check form-check-inline">
                                                            <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox"
                                                                   id="lv_@(group.Mode)_@level"
                                                                   checked="@_draft.LevelsOf(group.Mode).Contains(level)"
                                                                   @onchange="(e) => Edit(_draft.WithLevel(group.Mode, level, e.Value is true))" />
                                                            <label class="form-check-label" for="lv_@(group.Mode)_@level">@level.ToLabel()</label>
                                                        </div>
                                                    }
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="form-text">None checked = any level. @group.LevelHint</div>
                                            }
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Dice rolls ──────────────────────────────────────────
                       One checkbox per distinct roll, enumerated from DiceRoll.All — the lib
                       owns both the 21-roll set and its ascending canonical order (11, 21, 22,
                       31, … 66, the six doubles included), so the panel imposes no local roll
                       list or sort rule. Labels are the roll's own canonical token
                       (DiceRoll.ToString → "31"), rendered verbatim: display text stays owned
                       by the type, exactly as the enum facets defer to ToLabel. (A hyphenated
                       "3-1" presentation was considered; "31" keeps the panel free of any
                       label-munging and matches the lib's token one-for-one.) Selections are
                       held raw in the draft and handed to FilterConfig.Build() verbatim —
                       the panel derives nothing. None checked = facet off (Build() skips the
                       filter); the roll set is canonical/unordered, so checking "31" admits
                       both dice orders, and cube rows (no roll) never pass an active filter. *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.DiceRolls, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted">(none checked = all rolls; cube decisions never match)</small>
                        </label>
                        <div>
                            @foreach (var roll in DiceRoll.All)
                            {
                                <div class="form-check form-check-inline">
                                    <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox"
                                           id="dr_@roll"
                                           checked="@_draft.DiceRolls.Contains(roll)"
                                           @onchange="(e) => Edit(_draft.WithDiceRoll(roll, e.Value is true))" />
                                    <label class="form-check-label" for="dr_@roll">@roll</label>
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Position pattern ────────────────────────────────────
                       Per-location checker-range constraint, entered as the lib's raw bracket
                       list until the board-grid widget replaces this field in a later arc.
                       The text is the config's text, both ways (halheinrich/backgammon#269):
                       the buffer rides into FilterConfig.PositionPattern as typed and a
                       restored or loaded config's text lands back in the buffer as stored,
                       so a stored pattern a newer grammar rule refuses still shows the user
                       what they wrote. Validity is the lib's ruling like every other
                       field's: GetInvalidFields names FilterField.PositionPattern iff the
                       text is present and BoardPattern.TryParse refuses it, the input reds
                       itself on that membership, and the same IsCommittable member closes
                       Apply — so an invalid pattern can never silently drop, or silently
                       become, the filter the user meant to set. No BoardPattern is built
                       here for the config's sake; Build() parses, on the lib's side.

                       The help text below documents the lib's grammar — single-location
                       tokens over board indices and the named borne-off locations, and the
                       [a-b,min,max] range token (halheinrich/backgammon#268) — but does not
                       re-implement it: BoardPattern remains the single source of truth for
                       what parses. A wrong-signed bar or borne-off bound (say [off,,-2]), a
                       range with opposite-signed bounds or whose start is not below its end,
                       all fail TryParse and land in the same invalid-field state as any other
                       unparseable text, which is why the invalid-entry line names no single
                       cause: TryParse reports none, and a list of causes would be a second
                       copy of the lib's rules. The borne-off names render from
                       CheckerLocation's own canonical spelling, never as literals (see
                       Pitfalls); the example tokens are literal and pinned to parse. How the
                       opponent's at least n and at most n are written is Hal's ruling on
                       halheinrich/backgammon#275 (2026-09-25), stated here and in FilterHelp
                       alike with the ruling's own two examples.

                       Named by reference rather than from a label: the row's header
                       button already carries the facet's name, so aria-labelledby points
                       at it and aria-describedby points at the hint. The wrapper below is
                       a plain container now — its `for` is gone, because a label whose
                       text is the hint would have made the hint the field's name, which
                       is the lie this replaces. This was the panel's only named input
                       until halheinrich/backgammon#196 made the pair of references the
                       rule for every text and number box on the screen; it is the
                       precedent the others follow, not a special case. *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.PositionPattern, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted" id="facetHint_PositionPattern">(checker counts by location or range of locations; blank = no pattern filter)</small>
                        </label>
                        <input id="positionPattern"
                               @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(invalidFields, FilterField.PositionPattern, "facetHint_PositionPattern", "positionPatternFeedback"))"
                               aria-labelledby="facetToggle_PositionPattern"
                               placeholder="e.g. [6,2,] [5,,-2] [7-12,3,] [@CheckerLocation.OpponentOff,,-2]"
                               value="@_draft.PositionPatternText"
                               @oninput="e => Edit(_draft with { PositionPatternText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })" />
                        <div class="form-text">
                            [location,min,max] per location; blank bound = unbounded; positive counts
                            belong to the player on roll, negative to the opponent.
                            Locations: points 1–24, bar 25 (player on roll) and bar 0 (opponent),
                            plus borne off — @CheckerLocation.PlayerOff for the player on roll and
                            @CheckerLocation.OpponentOff for the opponent. A bar or borne-off location
                            takes no bound of the other side's sign.
                            [a-b,min,max] totals one side across locations a to b (a below b, bars
                            included): the sign of the bounds picks the side, the other side's
                            checkers there are ignored, and opposite-signed bounds are refused.
                            [a-b,0,0] = empty of both sides; [a-b,,0] = none of the player on roll's;
                            [a-b,0,] = none of the opponent's.
                            For the opponent, at least n is written with the upper bound — [1-6,,-1] =
                            at least one of the opponent's on points 1–6 — and a lower bound means at
                            most n: [1-6,-1,] = at most one.
                            E.g. [6,2,] [5,,-2] [25,1,] [@CheckerLocation.OpponentOff,,-2] [@CheckerLocation.PlayerOff,10,] [7-12,3,] [13-18,,-2] [19-24,0,0].
                        </div>
                        @* The panel's one feedback idiom — rendered only while it
                           applies, d-block, keyed to this facet's own field — so the
                           line exists exactly when the input's aria-describedby names
                           it. It used to sit in the DOM for Bootstrap's sibling
                           selector to reveal; a described-by reference to a hidden
                           element is read out regardless, which is why it moved. *@
                        @if (invalidFields.Contains(FilterField.PositionPattern))
                        {
                            <div id="positionPatternFeedback" class="invalid-feedback d-block">
                                Not a valid pattern — check each entry against the rules above; fix it to apply, or clear it for no pattern filter.
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>)
                }
            </div>
        </div>

        @* ── Apply and Clear ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
           The panel's two gestures on one row, Apply first and "Clear filters"
           immediately to its right at the flex row's standard gap — the
           placement ruled in halheinrich/backgammon#230. Clear sat alone above
           the facet rows from halheinrich/backgammon#153 until then, in the
           seat it shared with the Hide/Show toggle; halheinrich/backgammon#193
           removed that toggle, so the seat outlived its reason and the two
           gestures now read where the user commits, left to right in the order
           they are reached for.

           The flex row keeps both buttons shrink-to-content rather than letting
           either stretch, and it keeps the disabled-reason line reading as the
           line under the buttons, which is how the help describes it.

           Apply is gated on CanApply: the lib must name no invalid field AND
           the selection must differ from the last-committed config.
           ApplyDisabledReason explains the second case in the
           NamedEntriesPanel disabled-reason idiom (title + muted hint line);
           the first already explains itself in the offending field's own
           invalid-feedback line. Hoisted into a local like the active-facet
           set above: both surfaces of the reason read the one evaluation.

           Clear is the full-clear gesture under an honest name and is never
           gated: it clears filter values only (buffers to defaults, persist +
           raise the empty config) — it never touches host state, and never
           opens or closes a row, so it claims nothing about what is on
           screen. *@
        @{ var applyDisabledReason = ApplyDisabledReason; }
        <div class="d-flex gap-2">
            <button class="btn btn-primary btn-sm"
                    disabled="@(!CanApply)"
                    title="@applyDisabledReason"
                    @onclick="ApplyAsync">
                Apply Filter
            </button>
            <button type="button" id="clearFilters"
                    class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm"
                    @onclick="ClearFiltersAsync">
                Clear filters
            </button>
        </div>
        @if (applyDisabledReason is not null)
        {
            <div id="applyDisabledReason" class="form-text">@applyDisabledReason</div>
        }

    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Raised on <b>Apply</b> and on <b>Clear filters</b> — never per keystroke —
    /// carrying the committed <see cref="FilterConfig"/>. The same instance is first
    /// persisted to <c>localStorage</c> (see <see cref="ConfigKey"/>) so the panel
    /// rehydrates it on the next first render. A consumer that wants a
    /// <c>DecisionFilterSet</c> for in-memory filtering calls <c>cfg.Build()</c> on
    /// the received config; a consumer that ships the selection to a server
    /// serializes <c>cfg</c> as JSON. Required: the panel exists to produce this,
    /// so <see cref="EditorRequiredAttribute"/> makes a missing binding an
    /// <c>RZ2012</c> compile error rather than a silent Razor splat.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public EventCallback<FilterConfig> OnFilterConfigChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised after <em>every</em> gesture that touches the edit buffers — a
    /// control edit (typing, radio, checkbox), <see cref="LoadConfig"/> staging,
    /// Apply, and Clear filters — carrying the committed <see cref="FilterConfig"/>
    /// the buffers now equal, or <see langword="null"/> when they equal none. A
    /// consumer gating a downstream action (a Run / Start button) on "is the
    /// panel's selection still the one I acted on?" compares the payload against
    /// the config it last received from <see cref="OnFilterConfigChanged"/>; a
    /// <see langword="null"/> payload always means uncommitted edits are pending.
    /// <para>
    /// <b>Per-gesture, not transition-only — deliberately.</b> The panel
    /// re-reports the current answer on every gesture rather than firing only
    /// when the answer changes, and the reason is remounting. The committed
    /// config is component-instance state that dies with the panel, so a freshly
    /// mounted panel has committed nothing and reports <see langword="null"/> on
    /// the user's first edit — while a consumer whose own applied state survived
    /// the navigation is still holding a config from the previous mount, and so
    /// a stale "clean" belief that only this event can correct. A transition-only
    /// event is silent in exactly that state, because from the new panel's point
    /// of view nothing transitioned. Consumers therefore handle it statelessly
    /// and idempotently — assign from the payload, never diff it against a
    /// remembered previous one.
    /// </para>
    /// Required: gating downstream actions is what this panel's consumers use it
    /// for, and an unbound callback fails silently at runtime as a gate that
    /// never re-opens, so <see cref="EditorRequiredAttribute"/> turns a missing
    /// binding into an <c>RZ2012</c> compile error — see the silent-splat entry
    /// in this subproject's Pitfalls.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public EventCallback<FilterConfig?> OnAppliedStateChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The restored-filter notice's state (see <see cref="FilterRestoreNotice"/>),
    /// armed by this panel's first-render restore when it stages a stored
    /// selection and dismissed at the first buffer-affecting gesture or
    /// commit, or by the user closing the notice — one dismissal, held there
    /// and nowhere in this panel. The composite forwards the host's app-scoped instance — the
    /// lifetime that lets the notice survive navigation and die on reload by
    /// construction. The default is a fresh, panel-scoped instance so the
    /// panel is coherent in isolation (tests render it bare); only the
    /// composite's forwarding makes it §4-correct across remounts, and the
    /// composite's trap pin fails if that forwarding is ever dropped.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public FilterRestoreNotice RestoreNotice { get; set; } = new();

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised once per mount, the first time a <c>localStorage</c> call this
    /// panel makes is refused by the browser (halheinrich/backgammon#102) — a
    /// disabled-storage or hostile-privacy setting, which reaches Blazor as a
    /// <see cref="JSException"/>. The panel has already degraded by the time
    /// this fires: a refused read reads as nothing stored and the read site's
    /// own default applies, a refused write keeps the in-memory state, and
    /// Apply still delivers the applied selection through
    /// <see cref="OnFilterConfigChanged"/> and <see cref="OnAppliedStateChanged"/>
    /// — what the failure costs is only that the next visit will not remember.
    /// The host owns what the page says about it: whether storage is one
    /// page-level fact shared with the host's own keys, and the notice, are
    /// the host's (the condition notice under the umbrella's
    /// <c>SPEC-notices.md</c>). Optional, deliberately: a host that binds
    /// nothing behaves as before, and the panel keeps working either way.
    /// Raised outside the guard, so a host handler's own failure is never
    /// relabelled as unavailable storage.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnStorageUnavailable { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Single <c>localStorage</c> key holding the whole filter state as one serialized
    /// <see cref="FilterConfig"/> blob. The lib owns the JSON shape (<c>ToJson</c> /
    /// <c>TryFromJson</c>); this component never touches a JSON serializer directly.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>internal</c>, not private, so <see cref="FilterHelp"/> can render the key
    /// name in its "what the panel remembers" copy from this one constant rather than
    /// repeating it as a prose literal. Deliberately not <c>public</c>: a consumer must
    /// never see — let alone depend on — this panel's storage keys, which is precisely
    /// why the copy naming them is producer-owned and lives here.
    /// </remarks>
    internal const string ConfigKey = "xg_filter_config";

    /// <summary>
    /// The set of expanded facet rows, under its own key (camelCase after the
    /// <c>xg_</c> prefix, per the sibling convention: <c>xg_quizMix</c>,
    /// <c>xg_xgpPattern</c>). User preference, not filter state — it never rides in the
    /// <see cref="ConfigKey"/> blob, and <see cref="LoadConfig"/> / Clear filters never
    /// change it. The value is a JSON array of <see cref="FilterFacet"/> member names,
    /// written in row order; restore is all-or-nothing, so anything unreadable — not
    /// an array, a name no row answers to, a numeric token — restores every row
    /// collapsed rather than salvaging part of it. A display preference that resets
    /// once is not data, which is also why the key this replaced is neither read nor
    /// migrated.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// <c>internal</c> for the same reason as <see cref="ConfigKey"/> — see its remarks.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// This is the one value the panel does serialize itself, and the exception proves
    /// the rule: the <see cref="ConfigKey"/> blob's shape is the lib's, so the panel
    /// must never touch a serializer for it, while the set of open rows is the panel's
    /// own view-state and belongs to no other party.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    internal const string DisclosureKey = "xg_expandedFilters";

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the container over the rows is open — the one disclosure
    /// over all eight rows (halheinrich/backgammon#231). Its own key, beside
    /// <see cref="DisclosureKey"/> rather than inside it: that key's value is a
    /// list of <see cref="FilterFacet"/> member names and this container is not
    /// a facet, so a sentinel name in it would be the very corruption its
    /// all-or-nothing restore exists to refuse. User preference like the rows':
    /// never part of the <see cref="ConfigKey"/> blob, and
    /// <see cref="LoadConfig"/> / Clear filters never change it.
    /// <para>
    /// The value is the literal <c>true</c> or <c>false</c> and nothing else —
    /// one bit needs no serializer, which is why <see cref="DisclosureKey"/>
    /// remains the only value this panel serializes itself. Restore is
    /// <see cref="bool.TryParse(string?, out bool)"/>: anything unreadable
    /// leaves the container folded, the same posture a fresh visit gets.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>internal</c> for the same reason as <see cref="ConfigKey"/> — see its
    /// remarks.
    /// </remarks>
    internal const string MoreFiltersKey = "xg_moreFiltersOpen";

    /// <summary>
    /// What the container's toggle is called while the rows are folded away
    /// behind it — the words on the control, and the accessible name that
    /// follows from them.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>internal</c> for <see cref="ConfigKey"/>'s reason and no other: it
    /// is how <see cref="FilterHelp"/> names this control in prose without
    /// retyping it, exactly as that block renders a facet heading from the
    /// lib's <c>ToLabel()</c> rather than spelling the facet out. Deliberately
    /// not <c>public</c> — a consumer must never name this panel's chrome,
    /// which is why the copy that does is producer-owned and lives here.
    /// </remarks>
    internal const string MoreFiltersFoldedLabel = "More filters";

    /// <summary>
    /// What the same toggle is called while the rows are showing — the
    /// gesture it now offers, not the state the user is already looking at
    /// (<c>halheinrich/backgammon#239</c>). <see cref="MoreFiltersFoldedLabel"/>'s
    /// twin in every other respect, including why it is <c>internal</c>.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string MoreFiltersExpandedLabel = "Fewer filters";

    /// <summary>
    /// Which of the two the toggle wears right now — the rule that picks
    /// between them, owned here with the words themselves so the panel's
    /// markup and <see cref="FilterHelp"/>'s prose cannot disagree about what
    /// the control is called in either state. <c>private</c> because only this
    /// panel renders a label: the help names both and leaves the choosing
    /// alone, and no test needs the rule when it can read the button.
    /// </summary>
    private string MoreFiltersLabel =>
        _moreFiltersExpanded ? MoreFiltersExpandedLabel : MoreFiltersFoldedLabel;

    // The selection as the user is editing it — every box's text as typed,
    // valid or not, and every choice — held as one immutable FilterDraft
    // (halheinrich/backgammon#374). The parsed config and the lib's verdicts
    // on it are derived from this on every ask, never kept beside it, and an
    // edit is a new draft through Edit. Holding the text rather than its
    // config is what keeps a box the config cannot represent ("1.5" in a
    // move-number bound) on screen and marked, instead of rebuilt as no
    // criterion; see FilterDraft.
    private FilterDraft _draft = FilterDraft.Empty;

    // Depth facet — raw user intent as three per-mode pairs, held in the
    // draft verbatim and handed to FilterConfig.Build(), which owns the
    // clause-union derivation. The panel never maps toggles and level lists
    // to clauses itself (that SSOT lives in the lib; see the Analysis-depth
    // markup comment). A group here is the rendering of one pair: its mode,
    // its hint copy, and its level disclosure's session-only open/closed
    // state — navigation, never persisted (see the markup comment for why, in
    // contrast to DisclosureKey). The pair's values are the draft's.
    private sealed class DepthModeGroup
    {
        public required AnalysisMode Mode { get; init; }

        // Mode-specific hint line rendered after the shared "None checked =
        // any level." sentence — panel-owned copy explaining what "level"
        // means for this mode.
        public required string LevelHint { get; init; }

        public bool LevelsExpanded { get; set; }
    }

    private readonly DepthModeGroup _evaluations = new()
    {
        Mode = AnalysisMode.Evaluation,
        LevelHint = "The level is the evaluation's own.",
    };

    private readonly DepthModeGroup _rollouts = new()
    {
        Mode = AnalysisMode.Rollout,
        LevelHint = "The level is the inner level the rollout's games were played at — "
            + "checker plays never carry XG Roller-family inner levels.",
    };

    private readonly DepthModeGroup _bookRollouts = new()
    {
        Mode = AnalysisMode.BookRollout,
        LevelHint = "The level is the inner level from the opening-book entry, "
            + "when the book recorded it.",
    };

    // Render order = AnalysisMode declaration order (Evaluation, Rollout,
    // BookRollout). A property rather than a field because field initializers
    // cannot reference the instance fields above.
    private IReadOnlyList<DepthModeGroup> DepthGroups => [_evaluations, _rollouts, _bookRollouts];

    // The config this panel last committed — the one it raised through
    // OnFilterConfigChanged from Apply or Clear filters — and the reference
    // point for "has the selection moved since?". Plain component-instance
    // state, deliberately dying on unmount: a host that remounts the panel
    // (BgQuiz on a new folder pick) starts with nothing committed, which is
    // what re-enables Apply for the new folder without any host-side reset
    // call. Never persisted and never a holder: the first-render localStorage
    // restore *stages* a selection, it does not commit one, so a fresh mount
    // has no committed config even when it restores a full selection.
    //
    // A remount over an *unchanged* source is the one case where dying is the
    // wrong answer — the selection really is still applied, so Apply would
    // re-arm with nothing to do. FilterSurface reconciles that at its first
    // render through ResumeApplied, from the applied holder it mediates and
    // never from localStorage (see ResumeApplied and the composite's
    // first-mount reconcile).
    private FilterConfig? _committedConfig;

    // True once LoadConfig has staged an externally supplied config, or
    // ResumeApplied has staged the applied selection a previous mount
    // committed. The first-render localStorage restore checks it both before
    // and after its await, so a restore completing late can never clobber
    // either. Blazor's single-threaded sync context makes the segments between
    // awaits atomic — a plain bool is a sufficient guard, no locking.
    private bool _externalConfigLoaded;

    // Latched the first time a localStorage call is refused this mount, never
    // retried: storage does not come back mid-session, and a failed call per
    // key would be one identical console error each. Per mount rather than
    // app-scoped because this panel holds no app-scoped state of its own; a
    // remount rediscovers the condition at one refused call and tells the host
    // again, which the host's own page-level fact absorbs idempotently.
    private bool _storageUnavailable;

    // The facets that carry a row, in render order: FilterFacet declaration
    // order minus ErrorRange, which is always visible above the rows, and
    // minus the two UI-shelved facets (PositionTypes, PlayTypes), which an old
    // saved config can still activate but which this panel offers no control
    // for. It is the vocabulary of the persisted expanded-row set — a stored
    // name outside it is corruption, not a row — and the order the set is
    // written in.
    //
    // Deliberately NOT the source the markup loops over: each row's body is
    // its own markup, so the eight FacetRow calls and this list are two
    // statements of the same membership. FilterPanelTests pins them equal
    // (the rendered #facetToggle_* ids against this list) rather than letting
    // one silently outlive the other — a pin that is only meaningful because
    // the two are genuinely separate.
    private static readonly FilterFacet[] RowFacets =
    [
        FilterFacet.Players,
        FilterFacet.DecisionType,
        FilterFacet.MatchScores,
        FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange,
        FilterFacet.ContactTypes,
        FilterFacet.AnalysisDepth,
        FilterFacet.DiceRolls,
        FilterFacet.PositionPattern,
    ];

    // The stored name → row lookup, built once from RowFacets. Restore reads
    // names through this rather than Enum.TryParse: TryParse also accepts a
    // numeric token ("0" would arrive as Players) and every FilterFacet member,
    // row or not, so it would honour two kinds of value this panel never writes.
    private static readonly IReadOnlyDictionary<string, FilterFacet> RowFacetsByName =
        RowFacets.ToDictionary(f => f.ToString(), StringComparer.Ordinal);

    // Which rows are open. Default: none — every row starts collapsed, the
    // posture the hierarchy ruling asks for. _disclosureTouched is the
    // visibility counterpart of _externalConfigLoaded — true once the user has
    // opened or closed a row this session, checked before and after the
    // restore's await so a restore completing late can never clobber a fresh
    // user choice.
    private HashSet<FilterFacet> _expandedFacets = [];
    private bool _disclosureTouched;

    // Whether the container over those rows is open. Default: folded — the
    // same posture one tier up, and the state the container exists to make the
    // panel's resting one. _moreFiltersTouched is _disclosureTouched's twin,
    // and it is a second flag rather than a share of that one because the two
    // preferences restore from two keys: a user gesture on one must not
    // silence the other's late restore.
    private bool _moreFiltersExpanded;
    private bool _moreFiltersTouched;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender) return;

        await RestoreMoreFiltersAsync();
        await RestoreDisclosureAsync();
        await RestoreConfigAsync();
    }

    // The container's restore, RestoreDisclosureAsync's shape over one bit:
    // ungated by _externalConfigLoaded for the same reason (a host-driven load
    // stages filter values and must not move what the user left open), and
    // re-checking its own touched flag after the await so a gesture that
    // landed while the interop was in flight wins. Tolerant like the rows':
    // anything bool.TryParse refuses leaves the container folded.
    private async Task RestoreMoreFiltersAsync()
    {
        if (_moreFiltersTouched) return;

        var stored = await TryGetItemAsync(MoreFiltersKey);

        if (_moreFiltersTouched) return;

        if (bool.TryParse(stored, out var open))
        {
            _moreFiltersExpanded = open;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    // Deliberately not gated by _externalConfigLoaded: LoadConfig stages
    // filter values, but which rows are open belongs to the user, so a
    // host-driven load must not suppress restoring them.
    private async Task RestoreDisclosureAsync()
    {
        if (_disclosureTouched) return;

        var stored = await TryGetItemAsync(DisclosureKey);

        // Re-check after the await: a row toggled while the interop was in
        // flight is a fresh user choice and must win.
        if (_disclosureTouched) return;

        if (TryParseExpandedFacets(stored, out var restored))
        {
            _expandedFacets = restored;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    // Tolerant restore, all-or-nothing: the stored value must be a JSON array
    // of names this panel could itself have written, and one element that is
    // not discards the whole preference rather than salvaging the rest. The
    // strict reading is the honest one — the panel only ever writes row names,
    // so anything else is corruption or a hand edit, and half-honouring it
    // would restore a set the user never chose. Returning false leaves the
    // caller's default (every row collapsed) in place.
    private static bool TryParseExpandedFacets(
        string? stored, [NotNullWhen(true)] out HashSet<FilterFacet>? facets)
    {
        facets = null;

        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(stored)) return false;

        // Declared string?[] although the metadata is string[]'s: the two are
        // one runtime type, and a stored [null] really does put a null in it,
        // so the element nullability the check below relies on is stated
        // rather than assumed away.
        string?[]? names;
        try
        {
            names = JsonSerializer.Deserialize(stored, XgFilterRazorJsonContext.Default.StringArray);
        }
        catch (JsonException)
        {
            return false;
        }

        if (names is null) return false;

        var parsed = new HashSet<FilterFacet>();
        foreach (var name in names)
        {
            if (name is null || !RowFacetsByName.TryGetValue(name, out var facet)) return false;
            parsed.Add(facet);
        }

        facets = parsed;
        return true;
    }

    private async Task RestoreConfigAsync()
    {
        if (_externalConfigLoaded) return;

        var stored = await TryGetItemAsync(ConfigKey);

        // Re-check after the await: a host's LoadConfig or the composite's
        // ResumeApplied may have run while the interop was in flight, and
        // what they staged must win.
        if (_externalConfigLoaded) return;

        // Tolerant restore: a missing key, the literal null token, or corrupt
        // JSON all yield a fresh default config — no try/catch here. Only a
        // genuine parse arms the restored-selection notice: nothing stored
        // (or nothing readable) means nothing was restored, and the notice
        // must not claim otherwise over a defaults screen. An empty stored
        // config does arm it — the empty filter is still a filter and still
        // a choice the previous session made.
        //
        // The three outcomes are told apart here and nowhere else. Null is
        // nothing stored — an ordinary first visit, or a refused read, which
        // the seam already reported as the storage condition — and gets
        // defaults with no word. A document that is present but unreadable
        // gets defaults and the failed-restore notice: a failed restore is never silent
        // (Hal's ruling, halheinrich/backgammon#367). TryFromJson alone
        // cannot draw that line — it answers false for a missing key too —
        // which is why the null check precedes it. The unreadable document
        // is left as it is; the next commit writes over it.
        if (FilterConfig.TryFromJson(stored, out var cfg))
        {
            RestoreNotice.Arm();
        }
        else if (stored is not null)
        {
            RestoreNotice.ArmFailure();
        }

        HydrateFrom(cfg);

        StateHasChanged();
    }

    // ── The storage seam ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Every localStorage call the panel makes goes through these two, and
    // neither throws for a refused call (halheinrich/backgammon#102). A
    // browser with storage disabled raises a SecurityError on getItem and
    // setItem alike, which reaches Blazor as a JSException; unguarded, the
    // first read faulted the first render and a throwing write stopped the
    // commit before the host heard of the applied selection. The guard is
    // per call and translates the failure into "nothing is stored", a state
    // every read site already answers with its own documented default, so
    // nothing restates those defaults here. A refused write keeps whatever
    // in-memory state the caller had already moved.
    //
    // JSException only, as ExtractFromXgToCsv's BrowserStorage (the ruled
    // precedent, halheinrich/backgammon#91) catches: a serialization bug in a
    // caller, or a host callback that throws, is not a storage failure and
    // must still surface. The host is told once per mount, from the first
    // refused call, outside the catch — see OnStorageUnavailable.
    private async Task<string?> TryGetItemAsync(string key)
    {
        if (_storageUnavailable) return null;

        string? stored;
        try
        {
            stored = await JS.InvokeAsync<string?>("localStorage.getItem", key);
        }
        catch (JSException)
        {
            await RecordStorageUnavailableAsync();
            return null;
        }

        return stored;
    }

    // Reports whether the write landed: a caller whose claim depends on the
    // stored value having changed (the failed-restore notice's) must not
    // act on a write that was skipped or refused.
    private async Task<bool> TrySetItemAsync(string key, string value)
    {
        if (_storageUnavailable) return false;

        try
        {
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("localStorage.setItem", key, value);
            return true;
        }
        catch (JSException)
        {
            await RecordStorageUnavailableAsync();
            return false;
        }
    }

    private Task RecordStorageUnavailableAsync()
    {
        _storageUnavailable = true;
        return OnStorageUnavailable.InvokeAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Projects <paramref name="config"/> into the panel's edit buffers as a
    /// staging-only bulk edit — exactly as if the user had typed every value.
    /// No <em>Apply-side</em> effects occur: nothing is persisted to
    /// <c>localStorage</c> and <see cref="OnFilterConfigChanged"/> is not
    /// raised — the user (or host) still commits via Apply, and this method
    /// never moves the committed config.
    /// <see cref="OnAppliedStateChanged"/> <em>is</em> raised, like any other
    /// edit gesture, reporting what the staged buffers now equal: normally
    /// <see langword="null"/>, since a loaded config generally differs from
    /// what was committed — but the committed config itself when the load
    /// happens to stage exactly it, which is a genuinely clean state and is
    /// reported as one. Calling this also suppresses the first-render
    /// <c>localStorage</c> restore, so a config loaded during host startup
    /// cannot be clobbered when the restore's interop completes afterwards.
    /// Which rows are open is deliberately untouched — a staged config can
    /// activate facets whose rows are collapsed, which those rows' badges
    /// report, but opening one is the user's gesture, never this method's.
    /// Must be called on the renderer's synchronization context (any event
    /// handler or lifecycle method qualifies), typically via <c>&#64;ref</c>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="config">The configuration to stage. Read, never stored.</param>
    /// <exception cref="ArgumentNullException">
    /// Thrown when <paramref name="config"/> is <see langword="null"/>.
    /// </exception>
    public void LoadConfig(FilterConfig config)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(config);
        _externalConfigLoaded = true;
        HydrateFrom(config);
        ReportAppliedState();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Snapshots the current edit buffers — including unapplied edits — into a
    /// <see cref="FilterConfig"/>, for host-driven save-as while the panel is
    /// dirty. The gate is exactly Apply's validity gate, single-sourced through
    /// the same build path: this fails when
    /// <see cref="FilterConfig.GetInvalidFields"/> names any field — the
    /// state in which Apply refuses to commit, pattern text the grammar
    /// refuses included. It is deliberately no stricter than Apply either:
    /// both read the one <see cref="IsCommittable"/> member, so a rule joining
    /// the lib's field table (the score-token grammar did, in
    /// <c>halheinrich/backgammon#121</c>; the pattern grammar did, in
    /// <c>halheinrich/backgammon#269</c>) tightens both gates in the same
    /// edit. Save-as therefore captures exactly what Apply could produce, and
    /// no saved document is minted from a selection Apply would have refused.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="config">
    /// The snapshot when this returns <see langword="true"/>; otherwise
    /// <see langword="null"/>.
    /// </param>
    /// <returns>
    /// <see langword="true"/> when the buffers are in a state Apply would
    /// accept; <see langword="false"/> otherwise.
    /// </returns>
    public bool TryGetEditedConfig([NotNullWhen(true)] out FilterConfig? config)
    {
        if (!IsCommittable)
        {
            config = null;
            return false;
        }

        config = BuildConfig();
        return true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Drops the last-committed config so Apply re-arms on a still-mounted
    /// panel — the programmatic equivalent of the remount that "a new folder
    /// re-enables Apply" falls out of, for hosts that keep the panel mounted
    /// across a source change. The buffers, the persisted
    /// <c>localStorage</c> state, and which rows are open are all untouched:
    /// the user's selection stays on screen, it just no longer counts as
    /// committed, so <see cref="OnAppliedStateChanged"/> re-reports through
    /// the normal path (necessarily <see langword="null"/> — the buffers can
    /// equal no committed config once there is none).
    /// <c>internal</c> by design: the composite component is the only
    /// intended caller (tests reach it via <c>InternalsVisibleTo</c>) — a
    /// host never needs it, because a host either remounts the panel or hosts
    /// the composite, which owns the source-change rule.
    /// </summary>
    internal void ForgetCommitted()
    {
        // RaiseAppliedState, not ReportAppliedState: this is source-change
        // choreography, not a user gesture, so it must not dismiss the
        // restored-selection notice — a host whose source change crosses an
        // unmount runs no panel code at all, and the notice's fate cannot
        // differ by host mechanics. The restored selections survive a source
        // change untouched, so the notice's statement still holds.
        _committedConfig = null;
        RaiseAppliedState();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Resumes <paramref name="config"/> as the selection a previous mount
    /// applied — the mirror of <see cref="ForgetCommitted"/>, for a fresh
    /// mount over a source the caller's applied holder says is already
    /// filtered. It stages the config into the edit buffers <em>and</em>
    /// adopts it as the last-committed config, so the screen shows the
    /// applied selection and Apply does not re-arm over it. The committed
    /// config dies on unmount while the caller's applied state outlives it;
    /// without this a remount would arm Apply over a selection that is
    /// already applied, with the disabled-because-nothing-changed
    /// explanation missing.
    /// <para>
    /// <b>The buffers come from the holder too, never from storage.</b> The
    /// stored selection and the applied one agree only while remembering
    /// works: after an Apply whose write the browser refused
    /// (halheinrich/backgammon#102), storage still holds the previous
    /// selection, and a refused read holds nothing. A remount that hydrated
    /// from storage would then show one selection while the holder — and
    /// the host's gate — said another was applied. So this suppresses the
    /// first-render storage restore the way <see cref="LoadConfig"/> does,
    /// and overwrites it if it already ran: whichever lands first, the
    /// applied holder's selection is what the mount ends on. Nothing is
    /// written — the committed config stays unpersisted by contract (see
    /// this panel's Pitfalls) — and which rows are open is untouched.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <b>Silent, unlike <see cref="ForgetCommitted"/> — deliberately.</b>
    /// Forgetting is news: a gate the consumer believes clean just went
    /// stale, and only <see cref="OnAppliedStateChanged"/> can say so. A
    /// resume is by definition no news — the caller resumes from applied
    /// state it is already holding, so a report would only tell it what it
    /// just said. The asymmetry is the contract, not an oversight. The
    /// restored-selection notice is not touched either: an applied holder
    /// means a commit happened this boot, which already spent it.
    /// </para>
    /// <c>internal</c> for exactly <see cref="ForgetCommitted"/>'s reason: the
    /// composite component is the only intended caller (tests reach it via
    /// <c>InternalsVisibleTo</c>) — it is the party that mediates the applied
    /// holder and can therefore say what was committed; a host holds no such
    /// answer about <em>this</em> panel.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="config">The applied config to resume.</param>
    /// <exception cref="ArgumentNullException">
    /// Thrown when <paramref name="config"/> is <see langword="null"/>.
    /// </exception>
    internal void ResumeApplied(FilterConfig config)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(config);
        _externalConfigLoaded = true;
        HydrateFrom(config);
        _committedConfig = config;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    // The panel's cleanliness truth, computed in one place and consumed by the
    // two surfaces that need it — the Apply gate (CanApply) and the payload of
    // OnAppliedStateChanged — so the button and the event can never disagree.
    // Null means the buffers equal no committed config: either nothing has been
    // committed this mount, or the selection has moved since. Equality is
    // FilterConfig's own value equality, which is precisely why an edit undone
    // back to the committed values counts as clean again — a one-way dirty flag
    // would strand the user behind a disabled Apply in exactly that state.
    // Only a committable draft can match: a committed config always passed
    // the gate, and a draft the gate refuses is not the selection that was
    // committed, whatever it parses to. That is load-bearing, not cosmetic —
    // a box the config cannot represent parses as no criterion, so "1.5" in
    // a move-number bound over a committed config with no such bound would
    // otherwise compare equal and read as the applied selection.
    private FilterConfig? MatchedCommittedConfig =>
        _committedConfig is not null && IsCommittable && _committedConfig.Equals(BuildConfig())
            ? _committedConfig
            : null;

    // Apply's gate: the selection must be committable and must differ from what
    // was last committed.
    private bool CanApply => IsCommittable && MatchedCommittedConfig is null;

    // Why Apply is disabled — the NamedEntriesPanel disabled-reason idiom
    // (button title plus a muted hint line), except that here the panel knows
    // its own reason instead of being told it by the host. Covers only the
    // nothing-to-apply case: an invalid value already explains itself in its own
    // field's invalid-feedback line, and re-stating it here would be a second
    // encoding of the same rule. Null while Apply is enabled, so no stale
    // explanation lingers.
    private string? ApplyDisabledReason =>
        IsCommittable && MatchedCommittedConfig is not null
            ? "No changes to apply — this filter is already applied."
            : null;

    // Report what the buffers now equal, after a gesture that touched them.
    // Fire-and-forget like the edit handlers that call it: the panel has nothing
    // to await on the consumer's reaction. Every caller is a buffer-affecting
    // gesture — a control edit or LoadConfig staging — so this is also where
    // the restored selection stops being "the restored selection" and the
    // notice dies. Choreography that raises the event without a gesture
    // (ForgetCommitted) calls RaiseAppliedState directly.
    private void ReportAppliedState()
    {
        RestoreNotice.Dismiss();
        RaiseAppliedState();
    }

    // The raw raise, shared by the gesture path above and non-gesture
    // choreography.
    private void RaiseAppliedState() => OnAppliedStateChanged.InvokeAsync(MatchedCommittedConfig);

    // Guarded as well as visually disabled, matching NamedEntriesPanel's
    // handler-side gates: programmatic event dispatch ignores the disabled
    // attribute, and re-committing an unchanged selection would raise a
    // spurious OnFilterConfigChanged.
    private Task ApplyAsync() =>
        CanApply ? PersistAndRaiseAsync(BuildConfig()) : Task.CompletedTask;

    // "Clear filters" — the full-clear gesture: the draft back to an untouched
    // panel, then persist + raise the empty config (consumers treat it as
    // applied). Filter values only: the panel has no path to host state, and
    // which rows are open is the user's choice, not this gesture's.
    private Task ClearFiltersAsync()
    {
        _draft = FilterDraft.Empty;
        return PersistAndRaiseAsync(BuildConfig());
    }

    // The single commit point — Apply and Clear filters both land here, and it
    // is the only place _committedConfig moves on a gesture (ResumeApplied
    // moves it at mount). A commit is the other gesture that makes the
    // selection the user's own, so the restored-selection notice dies here
    // too (Apply is additionally the very consent the notice asks for). The
    // failed-restore notice ends here only when the write lands: its claim
    // is about the stored document, and a refused or skipped write leaves
    // the unreadable one in place, so the notice stays beside whatever the
    // host says about storage (Hal's ruling, 2026-10-07).
    //
    // The committed config is assigned before the write and both callbacks
    // are raised after it whether or not the write landed: the host must
    // hear of the applied selection even when remembering it fails
    // (Hal's ruling, halheinrich/backgammon#367), and the write is guarded
    // so a refused one costs only the next visit's memory.
    private async Task PersistAndRaiseAsync(FilterConfig cfg)
    {
        RestoreNotice.Dismiss();
        _committedConfig = cfg;
        if (await TrySetItemAsync(ConfigKey, cfg.ToJson()))
        {
            RestoreNotice.DismissFailure();
        }
        await OnFilterConfigChanged.InvokeAsync(cfg);

        // Re-derived through MatchedCommittedConfig rather than handing cfg
        // straight on: one computation feeds both the gate and the payload, so
        // the event can never claim clean while the button says otherwise.
        await OnAppliedStateChanged.InvokeAsync(MatchedCommittedConfig);
    }

    // Flip one row + persist the whole set. Not an edit gesture: no
    // OnAppliedStateChanged and no config write — the choice is remembered
    // immediately under its own key, independent of Apply.
    private async Task ToggleFacetAsync(FilterFacet facet)
    {
        _disclosureTouched = true;
        if (!_expandedFacets.Remove(facet)) _expandedFacets.Add(facet);
        await TrySetItemAsync(DisclosureKey, SerializeExpandedFacets());
    }

    // Fold or unfold the container + persist the one bit, ToggleFacetAsync's
    // shape and for the same reasons: not an edit gesture, so no
    // OnAppliedStateChanged and no config write, and the choice is remembered
    // immediately under its own key rather than at Apply. The two literals are
    // the whole wire format — see MoreFiltersKey.
    private async Task ToggleMoreFiltersAsync()
    {
        _moreFiltersTouched = true;
        _moreFiltersExpanded = !_moreFiltersExpanded;
        await TrySetItemAsync(MoreFiltersKey, _moreFiltersExpanded ? "true" : "false");
    }

    // The open rows as the stored JSON array. Written in RowFacets order rather
    // than the set's own enumeration order, so one arrangement of open rows has
    // one spelling on disk: a hash-ordered array would differ run to run for
    // the same user choice and make the stored value unreadable to a person
    // inspecting it in devtools, which the help invites them to do.
    //
    // Both directions go through XgFilterRazorJsonContext's source-generated
    // metadata, never a reflection-bound JsonSerializer overload: this
    // assembly is trimmable, and a trimmed host (BgQuiz) refuses the
    // reflection path at publish — halheinrich/backgammon#193 shipped it once
    // and broke that publish. The trim analyzer in this project's own build
    // now refuses it first, as IL2026 under TreatWarningsAsErrors.
    private string SerializeExpandedFacets() =>
        JsonSerializer.Serialize(
            RowFacets.Where(_expandedFacets.Contains).Select(f => f.ToString()).ToArray(),
            XgFilterRazorJsonContext.Default.StringArray);

    // Which facets currently hold active filters — the presence half of every
    // row's badge. Judged by the lib's activation predicates via
    // GetActiveFacets() on the draft's parsed config, the same build path Apply
    // commits through, never by re-inspecting a box or a config field here —
    // joined, by the draft, by any box whose text its field cannot be, which
    // is a criterion the user meant rather than none (FilterDraft.ActiveFacets).
    // Recomputed per render; the cost is a parse plus predicate sweep,
    // accepted over caching.
    private IReadOnlySet<FilterFacet> ActiveFacets => _draft.ActiveFacets;

    // The words on an active row's badge — the panel's display concern, and
    // the only half of the badge that is. It says how much the collapsed row
    // is hiding in the terms that facet is chosen in: a free-text or bounds
    // facet is simply "set" (a count of boxes typed in would tell the user
    // nothing they wanted), while a facet chosen by ticking options reports how
    // many are ticked. Never asked of an inactive facet: the caller renders no
    // badge at all in that state, which is why no member here answers "off".
    private string FacetBadgeText(FilterFacet facet) => facet switch
    {
        FilterFacet.Players or FilterFacet.MatchScores
            or FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange or FilterFacet.PositionPattern => "set",

        // A radio group: exactly one option is ever chosen, and the facet is
        // active only while that choice is not the inactive default — so the
        // count is one by construction. A literal because that is what it is,
        // not a number this panel could arrive at differently.
        FilterFacet.DecisionType => "1 selected",

        FilterFacet.ContactTypes => $"{_draft.ContactTypes.Count} selected",
        FilterFacet.AnalysisDepth => $"{DepthGroups.Count(g => _draft.Includes(g.Mode))} selected",
        FilterFacet.DiceRolls => $"{_draft.DiceRolls.Count} selected",

        _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(
            nameof(facet), facet, "Not a facet this panel gives a row."),
    };

    // The draft's parsed config — a fresh instance each call, so what Apply
    // commits and what a save snapshots share nothing with the draft.
    private FilterConfig BuildConfig() => _draft.ToConfig();

    // Put a config on screen: the draft becomes the editor showing it, values
    // copied, a stored value a rule outlaws kept exactly as stored.
    private void HydrateFrom(FilterConfig cfg) => _draft = FilterDraft.From(cfg);

    // One edit gesture: the draft it produced becomes the draft, and the panel
    // reports what that now equals. Every control's handler comes through
    // here, so no control can change the selection without reporting it.
    private void Edit(FilterDraft edited)
    {
        _draft = edited;
        ReportAppliedState();
    }

    // The verdict on every value that can be wrong — the lib's on the bounds,
    // the score tokens and the pattern text, asked of the same parsed config
    // Apply commits, so what the panel reds and what Build() would throw on
    // are one answer; and the draft's own on a box whose text its field cannot
    // be at all (FilterDraft.InvalidFields). Safe on every render: neither
    // verdict throws. Recomputed per render like the active-facet set, at the
    // same accepted cost.
    private IReadOnlySet<FilterField> InvalidFields => _draft.InvalidFields;

    // How an input wears the lib's verdict — the one place a field's validity
    // is rendered (halheinrich/backgammon#270), splatted onto every text and
    // number box on the panel. Invalid: the is-invalid class, aria-invalid, and
    // aria-describedby naming the field's hint and its feedback line, which
    // every site renders exactly while its field is invalid (so the reference
    // never names an element that is not there). Valid: the class alone and
    // the hint alone — nothing says "invalid" in any form, and nothing points
    // at a feedback line that does not exist. Only the attributes that vary
    // with validity, plus the class they ride in, so a site keeps its own id,
    // name reference and type beside the splat. Attribution stays the lib's:
    // the field asked for is the one the lib names, never a facet-wide red.
    private static IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object> FieldAttributes(
        IReadOnlySet<FilterField> invalidFields, FilterField field, string hintId, string feedbackId)
    {
        var invalid = invalidFields.Contains(field);
        var attributes = new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.Ordinal)
        {
            ["class"] = invalid ? "form-control is-invalid" : "form-control",
            ["aria-describedby"] = invalid ? $"{hintId} {feedbackId}" : hintId,
        };

        if (invalid)
        {
            attributes["aria-invalid"] = "true";
        }

        return attributes;
    }

    // The per-token detail behind FilterField.MatchScores, which names the
    // list without saying what is wrong in it. Asked of the grammar itself
    // (MatchScoreToken.GetFault) — the same rule GetInvalidFields swept over
    // the list, so the field the lib named and the verdict the panel words
    // cannot disagree — and read off the same BuildConfig() output every other
    // verdict here reads. Distinct faults only: the line explains a kind of
    // mistake once, however many entries made it.
    private IReadOnlySet<MatchScoreTokenFault> MatchScoreFaults =>
        BuildConfig().MatchScores
                     .Select(MatchScoreToken.GetFault)
                     .Where(f => f != MatchScoreTokenFault.None)
                     .ToHashSet();

    // The panel's whole validity truth, in one place: no field is wrong. The
    // domain rules are the lib's — since halheinrich/backgammon#269 the
    // pattern text's too — and the one rule beside them is the draft's own
    // about representation, so the panel keeps no rule of its own. Both
    // surfaces that must refuse an uncommittable selection read this one
    // member — the Apply gate and TryGetEditedConfig — so a save can never
    // capture a selection Apply itself would refuse.
    private bool IsCommittable => _draft.IsValid;
}
